INT. FIRE LOOKOUT TOWER - MOUNTAIN SUMMIT - DAY

A glass-walled cabin on stilts above a sea of pines. A cot, a stove, a Osborne fire finder bolted to a table, a radio on a shelf. The wind sings in the guy-wires.

NOELLE BEAUCHAMP (17, first summer job, sunburned nose, binoculars on a cord) sits on the cot, eating peanut butter from the jar. She has not changed her shirt in four days. The radio on the shelf crackles.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Tower Nine, this is Dispatch. Noelle, you there?

She leaps, drops the jar, grabs the mic.

NOELLE
I'm here. I'm here, Barrett.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
You sound out of breath.

NOELLE
I was eating.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Good. Eat. How's the view?

NOELLE
It's green.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Anything else?

NOELLE
No. Just green. And sky.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
That's the best kind of day, kid.

She picks up the jar, wipes a smear of peanut butter from her wrist, sits. The cabin creaks in the wind.

NOELLE
Barrett?

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Yeah?

NOELLE
Can I ask you something?

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
I'm not going anywhere. Go ahead.

NOELLE
Were you ever scared up here?

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Every summer for twenty-two years.

NOELLE
Of what?

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
The first time I saw smoke and wasn't sure.

She nods at the radio, though he can't see. She lifts the binoculars and sweeps the horizon. Her hand stops.

NOELLE
There's a gray thing.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Gray thing?

NOELLE
West ridge. A column. Maybe a mile.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Take a bearing. Use the finder.

She moves to the fire finder, bends, sights down the brass ring. She reads the number with her lips.

NOELLE
Two ninety-one. Two ninety-one degrees.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Distance?

NOELLE
Eleven hundred yards, maybe. I can't tell.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
You're doing great. Say it again, slow.

NOELLE
(dual with Barrett)
Two ninety-one degrees, west ridge, column of gray smoke, eleven hundred--

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio, dual)
^Two ninety-one, west ridge, gray smoke, copy, I've got a crew rolling.

They stop at the same moment. Static hums.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Was that your first call?

NOELLE
Yes.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
It was a good one.

NOELLE
Is it real?

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
We'll know in twenty minutes.

NOELLE
Twenty minutes is forever.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
That's the job. Twenty minutes of forever, and then you eat.

She laughs. She laughs again. She sets the binoculars on the table, sits on the cot, and pulls the peanut butter back onto her lap.

NOELLE
Barrett?

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Yeah, kid.

NOELLE
Stay on the line.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Wasn't planning on going anywhere.

She eats a spoonful. The radio breathes. Outside, a hawk drifts past the glass, and the smoke on the ridge thins and tears apart in the wind.

NOELLE
It's going out.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Is it?

NOELLE
It was a cloud.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Everybody's first call is a cloud.

NOELLE
You could have told me.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
And ruin it?

She puts her face in her hands. She is shaking, and she is laughing, and the tears are real.

NOELLE
I'm so embarrassed.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Don't be. You were right to call it. A cloud's a cloud until you prove it.

NOELLE
I called it fast.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
That's what you're for.

She wipes her eyes with the back of her wrist. She presses the mic.

NOELLE
Tower Nine to Dispatch. False alarm. Stand down.

BARRETT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Copy, Tower Nine. Good work, Noelle.

She hangs the mic on its hook. The wind sings in the guy-wires. She picks up the jar again, and this time she offers a spoonful to the empty room.
